[36m(RolloutManager pid=45201)[0m [2026-09-29 07:21:18.476 rollout_manager] sglang_rollout.py:473 - First rollout sample: ["<|im_start|>user\nThe profit from a business transaction is shared among 2 business partners, Mike and Johnson in the ratio 2:5 respectively. If Johnson got $2500, how much will Mike have after spending some of his share on a shirt that costs $200?\nPut the final numeric answer in \\boxed{}.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\nWe are told that the profit from a business transaction is shared **in the ratio 2:5 between Mike and Johnson**. That means:\n\n- Mike gets $2k$ part of the profit.\n- Johnson gets $5k$ part of the profit.\n- Therefore, the **total profit is $2k + 5k = 7k$**.\n\nNow, Johnson received $2500, and this corresponds to **$5k$ of the total profit**.\n\nLet’s find just the **share of the profit that Johnson received**:\n\n$$\n\\text{Johnson's share} = \\frac{5}{7k} \\times \\text{Total profit}\n$$\n\nIn this case, since **2500 is the value given**, we can directly recognize that:\n\n$$\n2500 = \\frac{5}{7k} \\times \\text{Total Profit}\n$$\n\nBut we know **Johnson received $2500**, and since his ratio share is $5k$, this implies:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5k}{7k} \\times \\text{Total Profit} = 2500\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n2500 = \\frac{5}{7} \\times \\text{Total Profit}\n$$\n\nNow, **we can solve for the total profit**, and then use it to find **Mike's share**:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total Profit} = \\frac{2500 \\times 7}{5} = 3500\n$$\n\nThen:\n\n$$\n\\text{Mike's share} = \\frac{2}{7} \\times 3500 = 1000\n$$\n\n**Mike will have $1000 after spending some of his share on a shirt that costs $200.**\n\n"], label: 800, reward: 0.0
